From b85ebe6c36a3db026af3820b1896061032033b1e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 14:16:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 088/206] refactor(shorebird): LinkedValue<T>, and the records
 it carries

ClassIdPair and PoolIndexPair were two hand-rolled structs standing in
for one template of theirs. LinkedValue<T> has no symbol -- it is a
template and every use is inlined -- but two independent element sizes
name it, each read out of the division libc++ compiles vector::size()
into: 112 against sizeof(ClassInfo) == 56 on the class table side, and 16
against sizeof(uword) on the object pool's. Both exactly double, from
unrelated instantiations.

The rename was the smaller half. The class table's link results carry
whole ClassInfo records rather than the two cids the mapper uses: their
stage 1 strides 0x70 and reaches the cids at +0x00 and +0x38, which is
sizeof(ClassInfo), not sizeof(intptr_t). We were narrowing at the point
of production and they narrow at the point of use, so our stride was 16
where theirs is 112. Following them makes the offsets equal rather than
merely equivalent, and it is not the dead weight it looks like -- the
records are what the producer already has in hand.

Three static_asserts now pin those sizes, so a change to ClassInfo fails
the build instead of silently striding past their layout.

The object pool's instantiation is LinkedValue<uword>, which their symbol
table spells out. The mapper takes signed locals immediately, because
everything downstream of it -- the mapping's -1, the taken bitmap's
bounds check -- is signed; only resolved indices are ever stored, so
GetBestMapping's -1 never reaches the type.

Negative control: swapping the two members turns nine tests red, which is
the check worth having here, since a base/patch mix-up in a pair of
same-typed fields is exactly what a rename like this can introduce
silently. Reverted. verify_all.sh is 7 ran / 0 failed / 137 passed, and
both ninja configurations build.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

